.TH DRAW3DKERNTEST 1
.SH NAME
draw3dkerntest \- check that the draw device implements the draw3d protocol
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B draw3dkerntest
[
.B -r
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Draw3dkerntest
draws the same geometry through both
.IR draw3d (2)
providers and compares the pixels.
.B /dis/math/draw3ddev.dis
writes protocol messages that the C handlers in
.B devdraw.c
execute;
.B /dis/math/draw3d.dis
does the arithmetic in Limbo. They are independent implementations of
the same formulae, so comparing their output compares an implementation
against an implementation rather than against itself.
.PP
That comparison is the only check on the first question worth having: a
device that accepts the protocol letters and rasterises them wrongly
passes any test that only asks whether it errored.
.PP
.B "It needs no window and no GPU."
Everything is drawn into an ordinary image and read back with
.BR readpixels ,
which depends on nothing outside the process \- that is what makes it
runnable on a bare board over a serial console.
.PP
Each primitive is compared on its own, which is what localises a
difference, and a checksum of each picture is printed so the
.B same
provider can be compared between a host and a board when the images
themselves cannot travel.
.SH EXAMPLE
Both environments agree exactly on the device provider:
.IP
.EX
host emu      poly 0x1124da01  line \-0x208b1d40  plot \-0x35ddb944
Pi 3B kernel  poly 0x1124da01  line \-0x208b1d40  plot \-0x35ddb944
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/draw3dkerntest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw3d (2),
.IR draw3dsafetest (1),
.IR qputry (1)
.SH "THE BASELINE, AND WHY IT IS WHAT MAKES THIS A TEST"
Besides comparing the two providers, the device's own checksum for each
primitive is asserted against a value recorded in the source.
.B -r
prints the three in a form that can be pasted back in, and does not
assert.
.PP
.B "Without that this harness cannot fail on wrong pixels."
It printed
.B "6 ok, 0 fail"
on a Pi 3B while reporting 228 differing bytes on
.B fillpoly3
and 18 on
.BR line3 ,
because the six were only the
.I "inked something"
checks and the comparison was informational. A backend assigned to
.BR devdraw 's
.B gpudraw*
hooks could have rasterised anything at all and passed \(em which is
precisely what the paragraph above says this test exists to prevent.
.PP
The baseline is the
.I device's
checksum and not equality with the software path, so it needs no
resolution of the provider disagreement below to be useful: it says the
pixels have not changed. Re\-recording is deliberate, and the rule is
the one
.B tools/ci/README
states for markers \(em a changed value fails, and the fix is either the
code or a reviewed new baseline, never a quiet pass.
.PP
The checksum is masked to
.B 31
bits, not 32, and that is not fussiness.
.B int
is 64 bits at runtime on this board while
.B %d
and
.B %#x
print a 32\-bit signed view, so a 32\-bit mask yields values above 2^31
that
.I display
as negative: a baseline pasted from that display is a different number
that prints identically, and the assertion then fails while the two
printed values match character for character. That happened three times
before the mask was narrowed. 31 bits keeps every value positive and
printable in both worlds and the baseline portable between the board and
the QEMU arms.
.SH BUGS
.B "fillpoly3 differs between the providers"
by 228 bytes of 4356 and
.B line3
by 18, and the test reports those rather than asserting them: the two
rasterisers disagree on an edge rule, which predates this and is not
something a comparison can fix.
.B plot3
agrees to the byte.
.PP
.B "This page used to say line3 agreed to the byte,"
and it does not. The figure was 18 bytes on every run measured.
.PP
The ink assertions are not decoration. The first version of this test
compared two
.B black
images and passed: a quadrilateral at
.B "z == 0"
is at the eye plane and clips, and the device provider queues its
messages until a flush. A comparison that cannot tell "identical" from
"both empty" says nothing.
.PP
It covers
.BR fillpoly3 ,
.B line3
and
.B plot3
only. The sprite letters, the matrix letters, the z-buffer letters and
.B fillpoly3g
are exercised by
.IR draw3dsafetest (1)
for safety but by nothing for agreement.
.PP
It says nothing about speed, and nothing about the GPU: the
.B gpudraw*
hooks are nil in the kernel, so what it checks there is the software
rasteriser.
